  <h1>What to Do If You Suspect Elder Abuse</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>When you first suspect elder abuse or neglect, the decisions in the first days matter. Steps taken (or not taken) in this period affect both the elder&rsquo;s safety and any later civil claim. This guide walks through the practical sequence. The overall principle: safety first, evidence second, legal claim third. A civil claim that produces a settlement two years later doesn&rsquo;t help an elder who continues to be harmed today.</p>

  <h2>Step 1: assess immediate safety</h2>

  <p>If the elder is in immediate physical danger or medical emergency, call 911. Do not wait for APS, an ombudsman, or an attorney; emergency response is faster and may be necessary to stabilize the situation. After the immediate emergency, the steps below resume.</p>

  <p>If there is no immediate emergency but you believe the abuse is ongoing, consider whether the elder can be moved to a safer location (home of a different family member, alternate facility, hospital admission if medically warranted). A facility cannot prevent a competent resident, or the resident&rsquo;s authorized decision-maker, from leaving. Be aware that in nursing homes specifically, federal regulations protect against retaliation for reporting or for the resident transferring out.</p>

  <h2>Step 2: report to Adult Protective Services</h2>

  <p>Call your state&rsquo;s Adult Protective Services intake line. If you don&rsquo;t know it, call the Eldercare Locator at <strong>1-800-677-1116</strong>; they will route you. Most reports can be made anonymously. Most state laws grant broad immunity from civil and criminal liability for good-faith reports, even from non-mandatory reporters.</p>

  <p>What APS does and doesn&rsquo;t do: APS investigates and (where appropriate) arranges protective services or refers to law enforcement. APS does not prosecute, sue, or litigate. The APS investigation produces findings that later become valuable evidence in any civil claim, but the civil claim itself must be brought separately through retained counsel.</p>

  <p>For nursing home or assisted living concerns specifically, your state Long-Term Care Ombudsman is a parallel resource. The ombudsman investigates resident complaints and advocates on the resident&rsquo;s behalf. Ombudsman programs do not have law enforcement powers but their investigations produce useful documentation and can drive facility-level changes quickly. Reach yours through the Eldercare Locator number above.</p>

  <h2>Step 3: preserve evidence</h2>

  <p>Before the situation changes, document what you can:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Photographs.</strong> Visible injuries (bruises, pressure ulcers, untreated wounds), living conditions (soiled bedding, unsafe environment), and the elder&rsquo;s overall appearance. Photos with metadata showing date and time are best; smartphone photos generally capture this automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>Written notes.</strong> Dates, times, who said what, observed behavior, your interactions with facility staff or caregivers. Contemporaneous notes are far more credible later than reconstructed memory.</li>
    <li><strong>Communications.</strong> Save text messages, emails, voicemails. Forward to a separate account if you&rsquo;re worried about losing access.</li>
    <li><strong>Medical records.</strong> Request copies of recent records. The elder (or their authorized representative) has a right to medical records under HIPAA. Hospital and clinic records often document earlier injuries that contextualize current findings.</li>
    <li><strong>Facility records.</strong> If the elder is in a nursing home or assisted living, request the resident&rsquo;s chart, MAR (medication administration record), wound-care notes, and care plan. The facility may resist but resident rights under OBRA and state law typically require access by the resident or authorized representative.</li>
    <li><strong>Financial records.</strong> For suspected financial exploitation, gather account statements, copies of checks, deeds, beneficiary changes, recent estate-planning documents.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Don&rsquo;t confront the suspected abuser before you have the evidence. Confrontation often accelerates record destruction and account movement.</p>

  <h2>Step 4: consult an elder abuse attorney</h2>

  <p>Elder abuse and nursing home litigation is a specialized practice area. The right attorney has direct experience with state Elder Abuse Acts, CMS F-Tag interpretation, life-care planning for elderly plaintiffs, wrongful death overlays, and (critically) the analysis of pre-dispute arbitration clauses in admission contracts. A general personal injury attorney can usually handle the case but may miss the statutory enhancements (attorney fees, treble damages, &sect; 15657-style pre-death pain-and-suffering recovery) that materially change the economics.</p>

  <p>Most elder abuse attorneys offer free initial consultations and work on contingency (33&ndash;40% of recovery; in states with fee-shifting statutes the fee structure may differ). They advance case costs. You typically pay nothing out of pocket. Bring whatever evidence you&rsquo;ve gathered to the consultation; the attorney will identify what additional records to pull.</p>

  <p>Time matters. Most states impose a 1- to 3-year statute of limitations on elder abuse claims, sometimes shorter for wrongful death. Some states require pre-suit notice to the facility. Records get lost or destroyed if not preserved early. Witnesses become harder to locate.</p>

  <h2>Step 5: address the arbitration-clause question early</h2>

  <p>If the elder is or was in a nursing home or assisted living facility, the admission contract likely contains a pre-dispute arbitration clause. Whether the clause is enforceable depends on multiple factors:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Who signed and whether they had authority to bind the resident.</li>
    <li>The resident&rsquo;s capacity at signing.</li>
    <li>Procedural and substantive unconscionability under the controlling state law.</li>
    <li>Whether the clause was a condition of admission (some federal CMS rules limit this for Medicare/Medicaid-certified facilities).</li>
    <li>Whether state-specific protections apply (some states bar mandatory arbitration for nursing home residents on Medicaid).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Do not assume the clause is enforceable. Do not assume it&rsquo;s not. An experienced elder abuse attorney will analyze this in the initial intake and may file a motion to challenge the clause before any arbitration proceeding begins.</p>

  <h2>Step 6: family communication</h2>

  <p>Elder abuse situations frequently involve family conflict. The suspected wrongdoer may be a family member; even when not, family members often disagree about whether and how to respond. Practical guidance:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Identify a single family point of contact for APS, the attorney, and (if applicable) the facility. Multiple voices confuse the process.</li>
    <li>Don&rsquo;t confront the suspected wrongdoer before legal advice. Confrontation accelerates evidence loss.</li>
    <li>Don&rsquo;t post about the situation on social media. Defense attorneys monitor social media; what you post can be used in defense at trial.</li>
    <li>Keep written records of family meetings about the situation. Decisions made informally are forgotten or remembered differently later.</li>
    <li>Acknowledge that the elder is the principal. Where the elder has capacity, the elder&rsquo;s wishes about how to proceed are paramount. Where capacity is impaired, the authorized decision-maker (POA, conservator, guardian) makes the call &mdash; not the most insistent family member.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 7: escalate within the facility if applicable</h2>

  <p>If the elder is in a nursing home or assisted living and you want to continue care at the facility while the broader process unfolds, escalate through the facility&rsquo;s formal complaint process:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Speak with the Director of Nursing or Administrator.</li>
    <li>Request a care plan meeting (federal law requires the facility to convene one on request).</li>
    <li>Document the concerns in writing.</li>
    <li>If unsatisfied, contact the Long-Term Care Ombudsman and the state survey agency.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Federal regulations protect residents from retaliation for raising concerns, filing complaints, or being part of an investigation. Retaliation, if it occurs, is itself an additional claim.</p>

  <h2>Step 8: financial exploitation specific steps</h2>

  <p>For suspected financial exploitation:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Contact the elder&rsquo;s banks and freeze further transactions where possible.</li>
    <li>Request a CTR (currency transaction report) review if large cash withdrawals are suspected.</li>
    <li>Notify Adult Protective Services and, where appropriate, local law enforcement.</li>
    <li>Consult an estate-planning attorney about challenging recent changes to wills, trusts, deeds, or beneficiary designations.</li>
    <li>Gather a forensic accountant if the scheme is complex; many elder abuse attorneys work with established forensic accountants.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 9: long-term planning</h2>

  <p>If the elder survives and the abuse is stopped, planning for the future:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Re-evaluate the living situation. Was the facility the right choice? Is an alternate care arrangement more appropriate?</li>
    <li>Update legal documents. If a POA was misused, revoke and replace. If estate-planning documents were altered under undue influence, work with counsel to set aside or amend.</li>
    <li>Address ongoing care needs. The injury from neglect may require ongoing rehabilitation, equipment, or assistance.</li>
    <li>Coordinate with the civil claim. The attorney handling the elder abuse claim should be aware of and coordinate with any criminal proceeding, APS investigation, ombudsman action, and estate-planning revisions.</li>
  </ul>

